---
import Split from './Split.astro';
---
<section id="how" data-tone="light" class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="g" style="row-gap:40px;margin-bottom:clamp(40px,5vw,72px)">
      <p class="eyebrow" style="grid-column:1/4">How I work</p>
      <Split as="h2" class="h-section" style="grid-column:4/12" text="The habits behind the three cases." />
    </div>
    <div class="principles">
      <div class="principle rv"><span class="n">01</span><h3>Start from the job, not the screen</h3><p>Every brief arrives as a screen request. I go back one step to the job it sits in. When a brief feels small but the problem feels structural, the job is to widen the conversation.</p></div>
      <div class="principle rv"><span class="n">02</span><h3>Get to users, whatever is in the way</h3><p>Twelve months with an ops desk. A year embedded with four teams. Paper tests with analysts. Clients watched deciding. My own kids at bedtime.</p></div>
      <div class="principle rv"><span class="n">03</span><h3>Three options, then narrow</h3><p>I bring three or four directions, mapped fast. People rarely know what they want until it's in front of them, and every no tells me where to go.</p></div>
      <div class="principle rv"><span class="n">04</span><h3>Decide what not to design</h3><p>Is this serving a real workflow contradiction, or one team's preference? If we're adding because we have a hypothesis, fine. If we're adding to feel productive, cut it.</p></div>
      <div class="principle rv"><span class="n">05</span><h3>Build it to test it</h3><p>If I can't prove a decision in Figma, I build it. The reversed decision in the FX case came from code, not a picture.</p></div>
      <div class="principle rv"><span class="n">06</span><h3>Accessibility is a gate</h3><p>Zero violations on Fusion. VoiceOver, Dynamic Type and Calm Mode in Winking Star. Shape and dash beside colour on every FX series.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
